{"turn":1,"elapsedMs":53732,"answer":"**IL6 is the gene; IL-6 is the cytokine protein it encodes.** IL6 gene expression is assessed through RNA abundance in specified cells or tissue. Blood IL-6 assays measure protein concentration—not RNA expression, DNA methylation, genotype or receptor signalling. [S1]\n\n**No: a blood IL-6 change does not prove changed gene expression in the brain.** [S1] Circulating protein reflects production, release and clearance across the body. Establishing brain expression requires evidence from relevant brain cells or tissue; even blood-cell RNA measurements cannot automatically establish brain expression.\n\n**Trust Factor crosses describe study design:**\n- **1 cross:** other peer-reviewed research.\n- **2 crosses:** randomized controlled trials, including unmasked or single-masked trials.\n- **3 crosses:** verified double-masked randomized controlled trials.\n\nA chapter/topic receives the highest tier supported by at least **three distinct qualifying study units**; linked reports count once. Chapter 14 has three crosses overall, but topic ratings vary. Crosses are **not grades of effect size, risk of bias, certainty or clinical benefit**. [S1] For example, the schizophrenia tocilizumab trial earned three crosses despite no demonstrated symptom or cognitive benefit. [S2]","chargedUpperUSD":0.8240625000000001}
